Research Grounding and Build Notes
Convergence is technically actionable when treated as a search-and-evaluation
system, not a single all-purpose prompt.
Key evidence
Gu et al., Generating Creative Ideas via LLMs for Scientific Research (2025) implement a two
phase pattern: knowledge preparation, then combinatorial generation. Their retrieval
represents innovations at multiple abstraction levels and their process decomposes
components before integrating them. The paper reports stronger alignment with later
research developments than its direct-generation baseline.
The same paper uses Bodenʼs distinction: combinatorial creativity recombines familiar
ideas; exploratory creativity searches a conceptual space; transformational creativity
changes its rules. The MVP begins at combinatorial creativity and leaves clear hooks for
the latter two.
Zhu, The Internet of Agentic AI (2026) frames networked agents as systems of planning, tool
use, communication, coordination, trust, and governance. Its useful concept here is
controlled emergence: allow novel collective behavior while bounding it with protocol,
resource, identity, and governance layers.
Practical decisions
1. Start with a narrow arena. Pick one mission class—e.g., climate adaptation projects,
developer tools, community health operations, or education—not “all ideas.” A sharp
evaluation function is an accelerator, not a limitation.
2. Store mechanisms and constraints. Plain documents and embeddings are insufficient for
creative transfer. Capture what a concept does, when it fails, and at least four abstraction
levels.
3. Separate four passes. Retrieve → decompose → bridge → criticize. Combining them in one
prompt encourages fluent but untested analogy.
4. Make disagreement an artifact. The critic must name what would falsify the bridge and
which stakeholder might be harmed or excluded.
5. Close the loop cheaply. Prototype with a simulation, code test, paper prototype, or small
user study. Save metrics and decisions back into memory.
6. Use evolutionary selection only a er measurement exists. Keep a population of
candidates, mutate/recombine high-scoring ones, but base survival on observed outcomes
rather than model self-grading alone.
30-day plan
Days 1–3: Choose mission, user/stakeholder, constraints, and one measurable outcome.
Add 30 curated atoms across 3 domains.
Days 4–7: Run 20 challenges through the included engine. Human-rate bridge quality,
feasibility, and usefulness; revise the atom format.
Week 2: Add an LLM adapter that produces structured JSON under the same card schema.
Retain deterministic retrieval and the critic.
Week 3: Add source-backed web/library ingestion plus a small graph database. Introduce
provenance and contradiction records.
Week 4: Build a protected prototype runner and a human review UI. Trial one reversible
candidate with real users or a simulator.
Sources
Gu, T. et al. (2025). “Generating Creative Ideas via LLMs for Scientific Research.”
arXiv:2412.14141. 
https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.14141
Zhu, Q. (2026). “The Internet of Agentic AI: Communication, Coordination, and Collective
Intelligence at Scale.” arXiv:2606.12835. 
https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.12835
Boden, M. A. (2004). The Creative Mind: Myths and Mechanisms, 2nd ed. Routledge.
